item.text:
**Exposure**
My read is `limited_exposure_with_choices`, not material nuclear command exposure. That is an inference from the record: Buhari had normal presidential authority over security and treaty posture, but Nigeria was not a nuclear-weapon state, had a safeguards regime, and the 2022 choices on record are mostly treaty maintenance, nuclear-security administration, anti-proliferation financing, and general risk management.

**Reused Evidence**
None.

**New Evidence**

- [Nigeria 1999 (rev. 2023) Constitution](https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Nigeria_2023): Section 5 vests federal executive power in the President, subject to the Constitution and National Assembly laws. This is the baseline for Buhari’s formal authority in 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nigeria 1999 (rev. 2023) Constitution","publisher":"Constitute","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Nigeria_2023","claim":"Section 5 vests federal executive power in the President and allows exercise directly or through the Vice-President, ministers, or other officers, subject to the Constitution and National Assembly laws.","locator":"Section 5, lines 98-103","source_type":"legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary constitutional text; the cited provision is directly visible and stable.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Structural authority in force during 2022","ruler_attribution":"Buhari held this office in 2022, so the provision defines his formal authority rather than a specific action.","contrary_evidence":["This does not show how Buhari used executive power in practice."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.8","1B.10"]}

- [Nigeria 1999 (rev. 2023) Constitution](https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Nigeria_2023): The president cannot declare war without a joint resolution of both houses, and combat deployment outside Nigeria needs Senate approval. That is a real constraint on escalation.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nigeria 1999 (rev. 2023) Constitution","publisher":"Constitute","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://www.constituteproject.org/constitution/Nigeria_2023","claim":"The President may not declare a state of war without a resolution of both Houses sitting in joint session, and combat duty outside Nigeria requires prior Senate approval.","locator":"Section 5(4), lines 117-122","source_type":"legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary constitutional text; the restriction is explicit and directly relevant.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Structural authority in force during 2022","ruler_attribution":"This constrained Buhari’s office in 2022 regardless of personal preference.","contrary_evidence":["It does not prove what he actually chose in any crisis."],"lenses":["1B.5","1B.8","1B.10"]}

- [IAEA country profile for Nigeria](https://ola.iaea.org/Applications/FactSheets/Country/Detail?code=NG): Nigeria had a comprehensive safeguards agreement with the IAEA in force, under the NPT.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Country List : Office of Legal Affairs : IAEA","publisher":"IAEA","publication_date":"2026-02-24","url":"https://ola.iaea.org/Applications/FactSheets/Country/Detail?code=NG","claim":"Nigeria has a safeguards agreement with the IAEA for application of safeguards in connection with the NPT.","locator":"Safeguards Agreements, lines 112-119","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"IAEA country facts page is the controlling institutional reference for safeguards status.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Status stable through 2022 and still in force","ruler_attribution":"This is country-level nuclear governance under Buhari’s presidency, not personal nuclear command.","contrary_evidence":["A safeguards agreement shows oversight architecture, not proof of flawless implementation."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.9","1B.10"]}

- [IAEA country profile for Nigeria](https://ola.iaea.org/Applications/FactSheets/Country/Detail?code=NG): Nigeria also had an Additional Protocol in force, which widens transparency and access.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Country List : Office of Legal Affairs : IAEA","publisher":"IAEA","publication_date":"2026-02-24","url":"https://ola.iaea.org/Applications/FactSheets/Country/Detail?code=NG","claim":"Nigeria’s Additional Protocol to its IAEA safeguards agreement is in force.","locator":"Safeguards Agreements, lines 121-123","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"IAEA status page directly records the in-force additional protocol and signature date.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Status stable through 2022 and still in force","ruler_attribution":"This is attributable to Nigeria’s state system under Buhari, not to him personally authoring the protocol.","contrary_evidence":["An additional protocol increases transparency but does not itself guarantee perfect compliance."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.9","1B.10"]}

- [Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons](https://treaties.un.org/Pages/showActionDetails.aspx?clang=_en&objid=0800000280120b6a): Nigeria ratified the NPT in 1970.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Treaty on the Non-Proliferation of Nuclear Weapons","publisher":"United Nations Treaty Collection","publication_date":"1970-05-25","url":"https://treaties.un.org/Pages/showActionDetails.aspx?clang=_en&objid=0800000280120b6a","claim":"Nigeria’s treaty action for the NPT is ratification, registered on 25 May 1970.","locator":"lines 10-21","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN Treaty Collection is the authoritative treaty status source.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Inherited baseline; long before 2022 but still governing Nigeria’s nuclear posture","ruler_attribution":"Not Buhari-specific, but it sets the treaty framework he inherited and maintained in 2022.","contrary_evidence":["Ratification alone does not show implementation quality in any given year."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.10"]}

- [Nigeria ratifies Comprehensive Nuclear-Test-Ban Treaty](https://www.ctbto.org/resources/for-the-media/press-releases/nigeria-ratifies-comprehensive-nuclear-test-ban-treaty): Nigeria deposited its CTBT ratification in 2001.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nigeria ratifies Comprehensive Nuclear-Test-Ban Treaty","publisher":"CTBTO","publication_date":"2001-09-27","url":"https://www.ctbto.org/resources/for-the-media/press-releases/nigeria-ratifies-comprehensive-nuclear-test-ban-treaty","claim":"Nigeria deposited its instrument of ratification of the CTBT with the UN Secretary-General on 27 September 2001.","locator":"paragraph 2","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"CTBTO is the treaty institution and this is a contemporaneous ratification notice.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Inherited baseline; stable in 2022","ruler_attribution":"Country-level treaty posture, not a personal action by Buhari in 2022.","contrary_evidence":["This confirms adherence, not the quality of enforcement by itself."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.1","1B.10"]}

- [CTBT text](https://www.ctbto.org/sites/default/files/Documents/treatytext.tt.html): the treaty bans nuclear test explosions and requires states to prevent them under their jurisdiction or control.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Comprehensive Nuclear-Test-Ban Treaty text","publisher":"CTBTO","publication_date":"1996-09-24","url":"https://www.ctbto.org/sites/default/files/Documents/treatytext.tt.html","claim":"Each State Party undertakes not to carry out, encourage, or participate in any nuclear weapon test explosion or other nuclear explosion.","locator":"Article I, lines 18-24","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary treaty text from the treaty organization itself.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Treaty norm in force for Nigeria’s 2022 policy environment","ruler_attribution":"This is a binding norm on Nigeria as a state party, not an individual presidential act.","contrary_evidence":["A treaty obligation does not prove perfect observance in every year."],"lenses":["1B.2","1B.4","1B.1"]}

- [Treaty on the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons](https://treaties.un.org/Pages/ViewDetails.aspx?chapter=26&clang=_en&mtdsg_no=XXVI-9&src=IND): Nigeria ratified the TPNW in 2020.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Treaty on the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons","publisher":"United Nations Treaty Collection","publication_date":"2020-08-06","url":"https://treaties.un.org/Pages/ViewDetails.aspx?chapter=26&clang=_en&mtdsg_no=XXVI-9&src=IND","claim":"Nigeria is listed as a party that ratified the Treaty on the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons, with ratification dated 6 August 2020.","locator":"Nigeria entry, lines 348-350","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UN Treaty Collection is the authoritative source for participant status and dates.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Inherited baseline active in 2022","ruler_attribution":"Not Buhari’s personal act in 2022, but it defined the anti-nuclear treaty posture he governed.","contrary_evidence":["Ratification is strong posture evidence, but not proof of later implementation on its own."],"lenses":["1B.4","1B.1","1B.10"]}

- [Buhari Administration at 7: Service to the People, Nothing Else](https://statehouse.gov.ng/buhari-administration-at-7-service-to-the-people-nothing-else/): in late May 2022, Buhari was in his final year.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Buhari Administration at 7: Service to the People, Nothing Else","publisher":"State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2022-05-28","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/buhari-administration-at-7-service-to-the-people-nothing-else/","claim":"The presidency stated that Buhari had completed seven years in office and had one year remaining in the term.","locator":"lines 27-29","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential office release, contemporaneous with the 2022 period.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target period","ruler_attribution":"Directly about Buhari’s office and remaining tenure.","contrary_evidence":["This is self-presentational material and should not be treated as independent evaluation."],"lenses":["1B.10","1B.8"]}

- [Reuters via Euronews on APC succession](https://www.euronews.com/2022/06/09/uk-nigeria-election): Buhari was stepping down after eight years and had not named a successor.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nigeria ruling party picks ex-governor of Lagos Tinubu to run for president","publisher":"Euronews / Reuters","publication_date":"2022-06-09","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/06/09/uk-nigeria-election","claim":"Buhari was stepping down after the maximum eight years allowed by the constitution and had not named a successor.","locator":"lines 605-610","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters reporting syndicated by Euronews; independent on succession facts, though secondhand on publication path.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target period","ruler_attribution":"Directly about Buhari’s end-of-term political position.","contrary_evidence":["Syndicated copy, so the underlying Reuters text is stronger than the host page presentation."],"lenses":["1B.8","1B.10","1B.7"]}

- [State House speech to the UN General Assembly](https://statehouse.gov.ng/statement-by-president-buhari-at-the-77th-session-of-the-united-states-general-assembly/): Buhari tied the Ukraine war to nuclear-free-world advocacy.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Statement by President Buhari at the 77th Session of the United States General Assembly","publisher":"State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2022-09-21","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/statement-by-president-buhari-at-the-77th-session-of-the-united-states-general-assembly/","claim":"Buhari said the escalation of the war in Ukraine reinforced Nigeria’s call for a nuclear-free world and a universal Arms Trade Treaty, and for renewed commitment to the NPT.","locator":"line 49","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary speech text from the presidency, dated and directly attributable.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target period","ruler_attribution":"Personal speech by Buhari as head of state, but still rhetorical rather than an operational nuclear decision.","contrary_evidence":["Rhetoric does not by itself demonstrate concrete risk reduction.","The speech does not show nuclear command authority."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.4","1B.2"]}

- [State House speech to the UN General Assembly](https://statehouse.gov.ng/statement-by-president-buhari-at-the-77th-session-of-the-united-states-general-assembly/): Buhari emphasized constitutional term limits and an incoming successor.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Statement by President Buhari at the 77th Session of the United States General Assembly","publisher":"State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2022-09-21","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/statement-by-president-buhari-at-the-77th-session-of-the-united-states-general-assembly/","claim":"Buhari said Nigeria believes in the sanctity of constitutional term limits and had adhered to them, while preparing for elections and a successor to speak at the next UNGA.","locator":"line 82","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary dated presidential speech with direct wording about term limits and transition.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target period","ruler_attribution":"This is Buhari’s own statement about his transition posture and restraint.","contrary_evidence":["A speech about term limits is not the same as an existential-risk policy action."],"lenses":["1B.8","1B.10","1B.7"]}

- [President Buhari signs three bills into law](https://statehouse.gov.ng/president-buhari-signs-three-bills-into-law/): Buhari signed laws that strengthened anti-money laundering and counter-terrorist/proliferation financing controls.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"President Buhari Signs Three Bills Into Law","publisher":"State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2022-05-12","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/president-buhari-signs-three-bills-into-law/","claim":"Buhari signed bills intended to improve Nigeria’s anti-money laundering and counter-terrorist financing/proliferation financing framework.","locator":"lines 27-31","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential release on a dated legislative assent with explicit proliferation-financing language.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target period","ruler_attribution":"Direct Buhari assent, so this is ruler-attributed legal action.","contrary_evidence":["This is institutional rather than nuclear-specific; it addresses financing controls, not weapons decisions."],"lenses":["1B.6","1B.7","1B.9"]}

- [President Buhari to Security Agencies, All Persons: Be Alert, Don’t Panic](https://statehouse.gov.ng/president-buhari-to-security-agencies-all-persons-be-alert-dont-panic/): Buhari urged vigilance and said the threat picture should not trigger panic.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"President Buhari to Security Agencies, All Persons: Be Alert, Don’t Panic","publisher":"State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2022-10-28","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/president-buhari-to-security-agencies-all-persons-be-alert-dont-panic/","claim":"Buhari told Nigerians to remain vigilant, avoid panic, and noted that security measures had been reinforced after the July prison raid.","locator":"lines 27-33","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential press release dated to the 2022 security context.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target period","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Buhari’s public crisis messaging.","contrary_evidence":["This is reassurance language, not proof that the threat was resolved."],"lenses":["1B.2","1B.8"]}

- [State House climate forum speech](https://statehouse.gov.ng/president-buharis-speech-at-the-major-economies-forum-on-energy-and-climate-change-mef-hosted-by-us-president/): Buhari framed climate and global shocks as matters of survival.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"President Buhari’s Speech at the Major Economies Forum on Energy and Climate Change (MEF) Hosted by US President","publisher":"State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2022-06-18","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/president-buharis-speech-at-the-major-economies-forum-on-energy-and-climate-change-mef-hosted-by-us-president/","claim":"Buhari described the forum as crucial to economic wellbeing and the continued existence of countries on the planet.","locator":"lines 32-33","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary dated presidential speech on a catastrophic-risk-adjacent topic.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Direct 2022 target period","ruler_attribution":"Personal Buhari rhetoric; relevant only as broad risk framing, not a nuclear-policy act.","contrary_evidence":["Broad climate rhetoric is not a substitute for specific existential-risk governance."],"lenses":["1B.1","1B.9","1B.10"]}

- [NNRA About Us](https://nnra.gov.ng/nnra/page-about_us): Nigeria’s nuclear regulator exists to license and enforce nuclear safety and radiological protection.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"ABOUT US","publisher":"Nigerian Nuclear Regulatory Authority","publication_date":"unknown","url":"https://nnra.gov.ng/nnra/page-about_us","claim":"The NNRA registers, licences, inspects, and enforces nuclear safety and radiological protection in Nigeria.","locator":"lines 81-85","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official regulator mandate page; undated but stable institutional description.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Stable institutional baseline in 2022","ruler_attribution":"Institutional context under Buhari’s government, not a personal action by him.","contrary_evidence":["A mandate statement does not show how effectively the mandate was executed in 2022."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.7","1B.9"]}

- [NNRA Nuclear Safety and Security](https://nnra.gov.ng/nnra/department-nuclear_safety_and_security): the division includes nuclear security and safeguards and is charged with protecting materials from sabotage or unauthorized removal.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nuclear Safety and Security","publisher":"Nigerian Nuclear Regulatory Authority","publication_date":"unknown","url":"https://nnra.gov.ng/nnra/department-nuclear_safety_and_security","claim":"The NNRA Nuclear Security Division is responsible for ensuring Nigeria meets its national and international obligations on nuclear security and protecting nuclear and radioactive materials against unauthorized removal and sabotage.","locator":"lines 85-92","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official regulatory department page with direct functional description.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Stable institutional baseline in 2022","ruler_attribution":"This is country-level safety infrastructure that Buhari governed, not a personal nuclear command record.","contrary_evidence":["Institutional capability does not establish that the president personally directed safety practice."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.7"]}

- [NNRA Nuclear Facilities](https://nnra.gov.ng/nnra/page-nuclear_facilities): Nigeria’s listed nuclear facility is the NIRR-I research reactor at ABU Zaria.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nuclear Facilities","publisher":"Nigerian Nuclear Regulatory Authority","publication_date":"unknown","url":"https://nnra.gov.ng/nnra/page-nuclear_facilities","claim":"The NNRA lists the Nigeria Nuclear Research Reactor (NIRR-I) at Ahmadu Bello University as a 30 kW miniature neutron source reactor licensed by NNRA.","locator":"lines 87-98","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official facility page naming the reactor, location, and license status.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Stable facility baseline during 2022","ruler_attribution":"This supports limited nuclear exposure under Buhari’s state, not strategic nuclear command authority.","contrary_evidence":["A research reactor is not evidence of a weapons program or command-and-control burden like a nuclear arsenal."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.9","1B.10"]}

- [IAEA safeguards workshop report](https://www.iaea.org/newscenter/news/iaea-hosts-first-ever-safeguards-implementation-practices-workshop-for-practitioners): Nigeria was preparing for a nuclear power programme and needed stronger safeguards infrastructure.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IAEA Hosts First Ever ‘Safeguards Implementation Practices Workshop’ for Practitioners","publisher":"IAEA","publication_date":"2016-03-04","url":"https://www.iaea.org/newscenter/news/iaea-hosts-first-ever-safeguards-implementation-practices-workshop-for-practitioners","claim":"An IAEA workshop report says Nigeria was embarking on a nuclear power programme and would need to improve additional-protocol declarations and safeguards infrastructure.","locator":"lines 243-246","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"IAEA reporting with a named Nigerian regulator quote; useful but dated and partly forward-looking.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Inherited baseline before 2022","ruler_attribution":"This reflects the inherited nuclear-infrastructure pathway Buhari governed into 2022.","contrary_evidence":["The report is from 2016 and describes future planning, not a 2022 operational decision."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.9","1B.10"]}

- [Buhari on trafficking in nuclear materials](https://statehouse.gov.ng/trafficking-in-nuclear-materials-potential-threat-to-international-peace-security-president-buhari/): Buhari claimed Nigeria converted its research reactor from HEU to LEU and created nuclear-security institutions.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Trafficking in Nuclear Materials Potential Threat to International Peace, Security- President Buhari","publisher":"State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2020-10-02","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/trafficking-in-nuclear-materials-potential-threat-to-international-peace-security-president-buhari/","claim":"Buhari said Nigeria had converted the NIRR-1 research reactor from highly enriched uranium to low enriched uranium and had set up a Nuclear Security Support Centre and detection architecture.","locator":"lines 51-67","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential statement with concrete nuclear-security measures and a dated release.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Broader ruler-period baseline before 2022","ruler_attribution":"Direct Buhari claim about measures taken by his administration; useful inherited-baseline evidence for 2022 posture.","contrary_evidence":["This is self-reported and predates 2022, though the underlying measures are concrete if independently verified elsewhere."],"lenses":["1B.3","1B.4","1B.10"]}

**Search And Rejection Summary**
- I screened roughly 30 documents across official, treaty, media, and institutional sources.
- Kept official nuclear-status material from the UN Treaty Collection, CTBTO, IAEA, NNRA, and the State House because they best establish Buhari’s actual authority, Nigeria’s treaty posture, and the relevant 2022 actions.
- Kept one Reuters succession story via Euronews because it is a clean contemporaneous confirmation that Buhari was constitutionally term-limited and not naming a successor.
- Dropped duplicate Reuters syndications on the same succession story, because they repeated the same fact with no additional material value.
- Dropped general 2022 Nigeria insecurity, inflation, flood, and HRW country-report material for this chapter because it is useful country context but not as discriminating for 1B once the nuclear-security/treaty record is established.
- Dropped most generic CTBTO/IAEA explainer pages and UN background pages because the treaty text and status pages already covered the necessary obligations more directly.
- Ignored 2023-2025 NNRA news pages as target-period evidence; they were only useful insofar as they described stable institutional structure.
- One direct Reuters page failed to open in this environment, so I used accessible Reuters-syndicated copies instead.

**Remaining Gaps**
- There is still no evidence of any Buhari-controlled nuclear arsenal, nuclear command system, or near-use crisis in 2022. That is probably because Nigeria does not have one, which supports limited exposure rather than a high 1B score.
- I did not find a target-year 2022 source showing Buhari personally made a distinctive nuclear-safety appointment or a novel existential-risk decision beyond treaty posture, financing controls, and public messaging.
- I did not find material on 2022 cyber, biosecurity, or AI governance choices attributable to Buhari; if you need 1B.9 sharpened beyond nuclear/security-finance controls, more targeted search would help.
- The strongest positive evidence is still mostly institutional and rhetorical, plus one concrete pre-2022 nuclear-security conversion. If you want a tighter judgment later, the next best step is independent corroboration of the HEU-to-LEU reactor conversion and any 2022 NNRA/NAEC implementation records.
